$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The Background</h2>
<p>In 2015, an infant aged about six months suffered severe spinal cord injuries in a motor accident, resulting in post-trauma myelopathy with paraplegia. Her disability was medically assessed at 90 per cent, permanent, with lifelong treatment and attendant care required. The claim was pursued through her mother. The High Court awarded Rs. 45,40,800. On appeal, a bench of Justice Ujjal Bhuyan and Justice N.V. Anjaria enhanced the award to Rs. 83,38,360 on 3 August 2026, with interest at 9 per cent per annum from the date of filing of the claim petition until realisation.</p>

<h2>The Principles the Court Applied</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>Functional, not merely medical, disability</h3>
    <p>The medical certificate recorded 90 per cent disability, but the Court assessed the loss of earning capacity at 100 per cent, because the child will never be able to undertake any income-generating activity. The enquiry is into what the injury does to the life, not what the certificate says in the abstract.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>A child is not a "non-earning person"</h3>
    <p>The Court rejected the equation of a child victim with a person never gainfully employed. A child's claim carries the whole of an unlived working life; notional income must therefore be fixed realistically, here at the skilled-worker minimum wage with 40 per cent added for future prospects.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>Multiplier of 18 restored</h3>
    <p>Consistent with the settled approach for victims below 15 years, the multiplier of 18 was applied in place of the 15 adopted by the High Court, the multiplier method being preferred as it accounts for inflation and longevity.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile">
    <h3>A realistic, humane approach</h3>
    <p>Injuries to children form a distinct category. Compensation must answer the deprivation across physical, emotional and social dimensions — including lifelong dependence on attendants and lost marriage prospects — and cannot be confined to conventional heads alone.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>How the Award Was Built</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Head of compensation</th><th>Amount (Rs.)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Loss of future earnings (Rs. 97,020 × 18 × 100%)</td><td>17,46,360</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Attendant charges (lifelong care)</td><td>25,92,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pain, suffering, loss of amenities and marriage prospects</td><td>25,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical expenses incurred</td><td>3,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Future medical treatment</td><td>10,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conveyance and special diet</td><td>2,00,000</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>83,38,360</strong></td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">
<p>Two figures in this table repay attention. The attendant charges — nearly Rs. 26 lakh — are the largest single head after general damages, a recognition that for a paraplegic child the cost of care is not incidental but central. And the notional income calculation starts from the skilled minimum wage on the date of the accident, not the unskilled rate that tribunals sometimes reach for by default in claims for minors.</p>
</div>

<h2>What This Means in Practice</h2>
<p>For claimants prosecuting a claim before the Motor Accident Claims Tribunals — including the MACTs attached to the District Courts in Delhi — the judgment supplies a checklist worth pleading expressly.</p>
<div class="check">
<p>Plead and prove functional disability through treating doctors, not just the disability certificate. Place the notified skilled-worker minimum wage for the relevant date on record. Claim future prospects at 40 per cent for a minor. Press the multiplier of 18. Quantify attendant care over a lifetime, future medical treatment, conveyance and special diet as separate heads, each supported by evidence. Seek interest from the date of the claim petition.</p>
</div>
<p>For insurers and respondents, the decision signals that undervaluation of child claims by resort to unskilled notional income, reduced multipliers or token attendant charges is unlikely to survive appellate scrutiny. The measure is the life the child would have lived; awards in this class of case are to be assessed with that future, and its loss, squarely in view.</p>
